Subject: content: Harden ForSecurityDropFullscreen callers (1/4)

WebContents::ForSecurityDropFullscreen() drops fullscreen across the
opener chain. On some platforms and configurations, exiting fullscreen
spins a nested message loop or synchronously runs event handlers, which
can destroy the initiating RenderFrameHost, reset the delegate, or close
the browser window out from under the caller.

This CL hardens the //content callers:

* EnumerateDirectory() revalidates the initiating frame after the drop.
  The pre-existing absl::Cleanup answers the FileSelectListener on every
  early return, so the renderer's reply callback is not leaked on this
  new bail-out path; the browsertest now asserts that explicitly. The
  frame pointer is also typed as RenderFrameHostImpl* so that a WeakPtr
  can be taken, and the non-null invariant its sole caller guarantees is
  now spelled out with a CHECK.
* ShowCreatedWindow() retrieves the delegate after dropping fullscreen,
  and null-checks it in case the drop reset it.
* ViewSource(), SetWindowRect(), MoveWindowTo() and ResizeWindowTo()
  null-check the delegate before using it.

Part 1 of a four-part audit of ForSecurityDropFullscreen callers:
  1/4 //content (this CL)
  2/4 //chrome mechanical caller hardening
  3/4 device chooser bubble
  4/4 cast dialog
A follow-up will add a continuation-based API (DropFullscreenAndThen) so
that callers cannot get this wrong by construction.

